L["rich-man"] = """Jesus told a story about two men whose lives could hardly have been more different. There was a rich man who dressed in purple and fine linen and lived in luxury every day. Purple dye was very expensive. He feasted well, and he did not seem to be a bad man in the usual sense. At his gate was laid a beggar named Lazarus, covered with sores and longing to eat what fell from the rich man's table. Even the dogs came and licked his sores.

This is the only person in any of Jesus' parables who is given a name. Lazarus means God helps. The rich man is never named.

The time came when the beggar died, and the angels carried him to be with Abraham. The rich man also died and was buried. In Hades, where he was in torment, he looked up and saw Abraham far away, with Lazarus by his side. So he called to him, father Abraham, have pity on me and send Lazarus to dip the tip of his finger in water and cool my tongue, because I am in agony in this fire.

Abraham replied, son, remember that in your lifetime you received your good things, while Lazarus received bad things, but now he is comforted here and you are in agony. And besides all this, between us and you a great chasm has been set in place, so that those who want to go from here to you cannot, nor can anyone cross over from there to us.

He answered, then I beg you, father, send Lazarus to my family, for I have five brothers. Let him warn them, so that they will not also come to this place of torment. Abraham replied, they have Moses and the Prophets. Let them listen to them. No, father Abraham, he said, but if someone from the dead goes to them, they will repent. He said to him, if they do not listen to Moses and the Prophets, they will not be convinced even if someone rises from the dead.

Why does this matter? The rich man's sin was not that he was wealthy. It was that he didn't see the man at his gate. He walked past Lazarus every day, and it never bothered him. Jesus is warning that comfort can make us blind to the needs right in front of us. And the story shows that this life matters and that our choices have lasting consequences.

What happened after? The last line was a quiet prediction. Not long after, Jesus would be raised from the dead, and many would still refuse to believe. The point was that Scripture already tells us what God wants, which is justice, mercy and care for the poor.

What does this mean for us today? Who is at your gate? It may be a neighbor, a coworker or someone you pass every day on the street. The story isn't meant to make us feel guilty about having things. It asks us to look up and notice people. Jesus always did. It also tells us not to wait for a dramatic sign. We already have what we need to know what to do.

That is why this story is so important. It says that God cares about the poor, and that how we treat the person at our door matters now and forever. And it points to Jesus, who left his heavenly home and came to the gate of our world."""

L["publican"] = """Jesus told this parable to some who were confident of their own righteousness and looked down on everyone else. Two men went up to the temple to pray, one a Pharisee and the other a tax collector.

A Pharisee was a respected religious leader who studied the law and tried hard to keep it. A tax collector was a Jew who worked for the Roman occupiers and collected taxes from his own people, often taking more than required. Everyone considered him a traitor and a thief. In the minds of the people listening, the Pharisee was the good guy and the tax collector was the bad guy.

The Pharisee stood by himself and prayed about himself. God, I thank you that I am not like other people, robbers, evildoers, adulterers, or even like this tax collector. I fast twice a week and give a tenth of all I get. Everything he said might have been true. He did fast and tithe. But notice that his prayer is really a list of how good he is and how bad others are. He wasn't asking for anything, because he didn't think he needed anything.

But the tax collector stood at a distance. He would not even look up to heaven, but beat his breast and said, God, have mercy on me, a sinner. His prayer was just one sentence. He didn't make excuses. He didn't compare himself to anyone. He simply asked.

Then Jesus said, I tell you that this man, rather than the other, went home justified before God. For all those who exalt themselves will be humbled, and those who humble themselves will be exalted. This was shocking. The man everyone despised was the one God accepted.

Why does this matter? The Pharisee did good things, but he trusted in himself. The tax collector had nothing to offer but his need, and that was exactly what God was looking for. The difference was not in their résumés. It was in their hearts. Jesus makes it plain that we cannot earn God's favor by comparing ourselves with others. We receive it by asking.

What happened after? Right after this parable, people brought little children to Jesus, and the disciples tried to send them away. Jesus said, let the little children come to me. The kingdom of God belongs to such as these. Humility and childlike trust are what the kingdom is made of.

What does this mean for us today? It's easy to read this parable and think, I'm glad I'm not like that Pharisee. And that is the trap, because then we are praying the Pharisee's prayer. The better question is whether we have been comparing ourselves to others to feel better. The tax collector's prayer is available to anyone, at any time. It is short, and it is honest. God, have mercy on me, a sinner. Some Christians pray it slowly, over and over, as a daily habit.

That is why this story is so important. It says that God welcomes the humble and not the proud. And it points to Jesus, who made the mercy that this man prayed for possible."""
